I wrote my conduct-patch (http://home.arcor.de/adorn/) to be able to do 
these kind of things. It makes illiterate characters illiterate, allows 
to start nude and blindfolded, allows pacifists to quit after killing 
something (going directly to DYWYPI seemed a bit harsh)...

In my patch the player can set some options to tell what he want's to do 
(at character creation). So you get told at the end of the game if you 
failed.

There are also some roleplay intrinsics (e.g. literacy, atheism). These 
get treated like gender/alignment. They're set at character creation, 
it's hard to change them during the game, and they have some effects 
during gameplay.

In case anyone has suggestions for in-game special treatment for 
conduct-characters: Tell me. I just updated my patch for NH3.4.2, and 
would like to improve it a bit.

E.g. special treatment for atheists is missing (special messages, ways 
to follow/abandon a god...). Next I'll probably add some temporary 
illiteracy (caused by amnesia)...

> Now *that* is a cool idea... though I think much of the interest in these
> conducts is having the ability to break them, and choosing not to.  For
> example, a recent wishless genoless character of mine spent a long time
> debating whether to break a conduct -- it made for an interesting choice.
> If I was forced to retain an initial conduct, I'd lose that interesting
> choice, which is a bad thing.

For wishless, genoless, polyless it makes sense to have a choice, but 
e.g. for illiteracy it absolutely doesn't, at least gaining/losing 
literacy should be a rare thing (like gender/alignment change).

Anyway I don't think it makes much sense to add an option to enforce 
wishless conduct. To me it's:
   wishless = "lucky or patient enough to wait for everything you need"
   enforced wishless = "intentionally lucky or intentionally patient"
And I don't think there are many useful things an option for wishless 
could do.

